WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.960
OK, first let's wind things back and

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.760
talk about Omar al-Bashir. He took power

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.320
in a military coup in 1989 and ruled

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:59.800
Sudan for the next 30 years. But two

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.800
years ago people finally turned on him.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.400
Women, young people, Sudanese from across

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.760
the country had had enough.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.320
And when it became clear that

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.400
al-Bashir's days were numbered the army,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.840
which had propped him up for so long,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.640
turned on him, too.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.640
And he was out.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.360
But it wasn't quite the people's victory

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.360
that all those protesters had wanted

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.200
because the army seized power and

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.680
replaced al-Bashir with something called

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.040
the Transitional Military Council, the

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.560
TMC.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.560
So, as far as the protesters were

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.560
concerned, the revolution wasn't over.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.640
But there was nothing peaceful about what

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:23.720
happened next. 

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.200
More than a hundred people were killed

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.040
in what became known as the Khartoum

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.840
massacre. 

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.760
But still the protesters kept up the pressure and

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.480
it worked. Eventually the military agreed

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.360
to share power.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.040
So the military leaders, the TMC, made a

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.360
deal with the group that basically

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.480
represented the protesters. That's the

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:07.120
FFC, the Forces of Freedom and Change.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.120
They agreed to share power for 39 months

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.840
in a new transitional government and

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.120
then hold elections. Both sides had seats

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.040
on a joint committee called the

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.440
Sovereign Council that was led by

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.599
General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan. Remember,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.080
he's the guy who just led the coup. There

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:24.400
was also a civilian cabinet led by Prime

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.720
Minister Abdulla Hamdok.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.720
Now, for a while it looked like Sudan was

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.040
on track and transitioning towards

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.120
democracy, but that transition was

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.519
completely derailed by what happened in

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:39.040
late October, when General al-Burhan

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:39.040
decided to seize power.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.280
He dissolved the Sovereign Council,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.680
pushed out the prime minister and

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.720
declared a state of emergency. 

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.239
OK, but why now?

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:04.400
Well, that whole power-sharing

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:06.879
arrangement was on pretty shaky ground

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.879
from the start. 

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.280
So, that's the context: a major rivalry between the

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.520
two sides that was just getting worse

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.320
and worse. But there was something else

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.320
going on, too. A looming deadline for

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.320
General al-Burhan to step down as

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.320
chairman of the Sovereign Council. 

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.560
Some people reckon that also helps to

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.040
explain the timing of this coup. 

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.199
And stepping down would have

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.600
put the civilian side in a stronger

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:03.759
position to make some big moves, like

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.759
handing over the former president, Omar

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:08.320
al-Bashir, to the International Criminal

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:10.880
Court. He's facing charges linked to the

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.600
conflict in Darfur. A conflict that some

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.360
of those military leaders, like al-Burhan,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.400
were involved in, too.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.880
Or another big move: pushing ahead with a

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.960
proper investigation into the military's

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.120
role in the Khartoum massacre.

00:05:27.039 --> 00:05:33.039
The military has full control

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.240
now. So, what does that mean for Sudan?

00:05:33.039 --> 00:05:38.240
Well, for the economy it's definitely bad.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.320
Sudan was on its way to getting $50 billion

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.960
in debt relief. And there

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.840
were promises of even more international

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.759
aid.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.759
But most of that was tied to Sudan's

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:50.639
democratic transition, and now that

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:51.520
that's in doubt, so is the money. 

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:22.479
Now, General al-Burhan actually says he's

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.240
still committed to that transition. He

00:06:22.479 --> 00:06:26.319
said the whole reason he took control

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.800
was to "correct the path of the

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:30.880
revolution", as he put it, and that the

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.680
military was forced to act because of

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.680
all the political infighting.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.280
He says he's going to set up a

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:52.639
technocratic government and hold

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:55.520
elections in 2023. But people just aren't

00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:57.759
buying it. 

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:06.880
And so, for

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:10.120
many in Sudan, it's feeling like 2019 all

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.120
over again.

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:23.199
We can't predict what might happen next

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:25.120
in Sudan. Several people have already

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:27.680
been killed in the protests that followed

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.960
the latest coup, and there's definitely a

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.680
lot of fear.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:34.319
But there's also some hope, the idea that

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.000
people power has changed things before

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:37.919
and it might again.

